#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068571 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "c1dcb0a7-441f-5628-ba23-71d43d9ea96c" :question/math-academy-id "e-495" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int x^2\\cos(3x) \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "In this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={x}^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=2x\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula for integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({x}^{2})(\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))-\\int (\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))2x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAgain, we use integration by parts to evaluate $\\displaystyle \\int x\\sin(3x) \\textrm{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=x & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=1\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x) & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x=-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =x(-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))-\\int (-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))\\cdot 1\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}\\int \\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{3}(\\frac{1}{3}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x))+{C}_{1} \\\\  & =-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+{C}_{1}\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is a constant of integration.\nSo we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2}{3}(-\\,\\frac{1}{3}\\,x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)+\\frac{1}{9}\\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}{x}^{2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+\\frac{2}{9}x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2}{27}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)-\\frac{2{C}_{1}}{3} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{27}((9{x}^{2}-2)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)+6x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x))+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -\\dfrac{2C_1}{3}.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068572 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "b81ff533-2067-5d1a-b265-df1ad91fa8da" :question/math-academy-id "e-496" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_1^e 32x^3(\\ln x)^2 \\textrm{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=32{x}^{3} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int 32{x}^{3}\\text{d}x=8{x}^{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 32{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}(8{x}^{4})-\\int (8{x}^{4})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int {x^3\\ln(x)} \\textrm{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}={x}^{3} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int {x}^{3}\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)(\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{4}}{4})(\\frac{1}{x})\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{1}{4}\\int {x}^{3}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}+{C}_{1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C_1$ is an integral constant. So, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 32{x}^{3}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)^{2}\\text{d}x & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16\\int {x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-16(\\frac{{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)}{4}-\\frac{{x}^{4}}{16}+{C}_{1}) \\\\  & =8{x}^{4}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4{x}^{4}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+{x}^{4}-16{C}_{1} \\\\  & ={x}^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)+C\\,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -4C_1.$ Finally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}32{x}^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)\\text{d}x & ={{x}^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡x)}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x)+1)|}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =[(e)^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e))}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(e)+1)]-[(1)^{4}(8{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1))}^{2}-4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(1)+1)] \\\\  & =[{e}^{4}(8-4+1)]-1 \\\\  & =5{e}^{4}-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068636 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/id #uuid "8ec71af3-4534-5ef1-8745-3220102ba838" :question/math-academy-id "e-7053" :question/problem "Evaluate the integral $\\displaystyle \\int_0^1 (t^2+1)e^{2t+1} \\, \\textrm{d} t.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={t}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{2t+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2}+1)(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\displaystyle\\int t\\,e^{2t+1}\\,\\textrm{d}t.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{2t+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t)(\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\int t\\,{e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-(\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}}{2}-\\frac{t\\,{e}^{2t+1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}(2{t}^{2}-2t+3)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C = -{D}.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}({t}^{2}+1){e}^{2t+1}\\,\\text{d}t & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2t+1}}{4}(2{t}^{2}-2t+3)]}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(1)+1}}{4}(2(1)^{2}-2(1)+3))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)+1}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)+3)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4}(2-2+3)-\\frac{e}{4}(0-0+3) \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{3e}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{3}-3e}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186250319 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "082f5ffa-db8f-5b86-9f1f-66bec8888277" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2{e}^{2}-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247495} {:answer/id #uuid "312a7284-86a2-58d1-925a-aae469b9cc31" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}+2}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186247869} {:answer/id #uuid "4646f294-569b-5c9a-8e3f-8b4bffc32dd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1-2e}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248052} {:answer/id #uuid "8a1ab61f-f876-54ff-ac9a-719572398e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248654} {:answer/id #uuid "aed8ce21-2fef-51ea-932a-bf3d76234eac" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8e" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248988}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "8a1ab61f-f876-54ff-ac9a-719572398e93" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186248654} :answer-field/id #uuid "90297971-15bc-5633-a065-ce4100255f03" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186249928}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "dca36867-5a6f-51bd-9e64-ad043dde12a1" :question/math-academy-id "q-114900" :question/problem "Evaluate ${\\int }_{1}^{e}4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=4x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 4x\\,\\text{d}x=2{x}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThis gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={({log}_{3}⁡x)}^{2}(2{x}^{2})-\\int (2{x}^{2})(\\frac{2{log}_{3}⁡x}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int x{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int x{log}_{3}⁡(x)\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={log}_{3}⁡(x)\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3} \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=x\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int x\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{log}_{3}⁡x\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =({log}_{3}⁡x)(\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{x}^{2}}{2})(\\frac{1}{x\\operatorname{ln}⁡3})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{1}{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}\\int x\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}(\\frac{{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{2}-\\frac{{x}^{2}}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+D) \\\\  & =2{x}^{2}({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}{log}_{3}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{2{x}^{2}{ln}^{2}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}-\\frac{2{x}^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-\\frac{4D}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$ \n\nNote that in the above calculation we used the change of base formula ${log}_{3}⁡x=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡x}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}.$\n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{e}4x({log}_{3}⁡x)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & ={[\\frac{{x}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡x-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡x+1)]}_{1}^{e} \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡e-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+1)-\\frac{{1}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2{ln}^{2}⁡1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(2-2+1)-\\frac{1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}(0-0+1) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2}-1}{{ln}^{2}⁡3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320933 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6b075ced-5a58-52b1-9238-36dc27c2ec56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{e}^{4}-3{e}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319719}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6b075ced-5a58-52b1-9238-36dc27c2ec56" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3{e}^{4}-3{e}^{2}}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319719} :answer-field/id #uuid "0d64743d-9a58-56a2-86a7-e957db42f825" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320187}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "1a2f1472-983a-5988-9015-40ac8b729fa5" :question/math-academy-id "q-351983" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={t}^{2}\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=2t, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}=3{e}^{2t+2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int 3{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2t+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =({t}^{2})(\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2t+2})-\\int (\\frac{3}{2}{e}^{2t+2})(2t)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}-3\\int t{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int t{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=t\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}=1, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}={e}^{2t+2}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t=\\int {e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t=\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t+2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int t{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}t}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =(t)(\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t+2})-\\int (\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{2t+2})(1)\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{2t+2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{2t+2}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2t+2}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}-3\\int t{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}-3(\\frac{1}{2}t{e}^{2t+2}-\\frac{1}{4}{e}^{2t+2}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}-\\frac{3}{2}t{e}^{2t+2}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2t+2}+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-3D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{1}3{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}\\,\\text{d}t & =(\\frac{3}{2}{t}^{2}{e}^{2t+2}-\\frac{3}{2}t{e}^{2t+2}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2t+2})\\,{|}_{0}^{1} \\\\  & =(\\frac{3}{2}({1}^{2}){e}^{2(1)+2}-\\frac{3}{2}(1){e}^{2(1)+2}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2(1)+2})-(\\frac{3}{2}({0}^{2}){e}^{2(0)+2}-\\frac{3}{2}(0){e}^{2(0)+2}+\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2(0)+2}) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{4}-\\frac{3}{4}{e}^{2} \\\\  & =\\frac{3{e}^{4}-3{e}^{2}}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320934 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "823c5e0e-13c8-50c5-9177-f0541f81db79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11{e}^{3}-7e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319786}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "823c5e0e-13c8-50c5-9177-f0541f81db79" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{11{e}^{3}-7e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319786} :answer-field/id #uuid "f0269145-0ae9-55e4-a07c-12dcf8ceee64" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320668}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "e235b03c-6338-5aac-b78a-cee6da78b778" :question/math-academy-id "q-351991" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1}^{2}({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={y}^{2}+3\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=2y, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}={e}^{2y-1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y=\\int {e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y=\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =({y}^{2}+3)(\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2})(2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\int y\\,{e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int y\\,{e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=y\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}={e}^{2y-1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y=\\int {e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y=\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,{e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(y)(\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\int y\\,{e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}}{2}-(\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}}{2}-\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y-1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}(2{y}^{2}-2y+7)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1}^{2}({y}^{2}+3){e}^{2y-1}\\,\\text{d}y & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2y-1}}{4}(2{y}^{2}-2y+7)]}_{1}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(2)-1}}{4}(2(2)^{2}-2(2)+7))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(1)-1}}{4}(2(1)^{2}-2(1)+7)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{3}}{4}(8-4+7)-\\frac{e}{4}(2-2+7) \\\\  & =\\frac{11{e}^{3}}{4}-\\frac{7e}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{11{e}^{3}-7e}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320935 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068919 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b69f026a-50b3-505d-bd9d-8b295691b66e" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of an Exponential Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ec3dfe4a-5ecf-52b0-bf9f-2434195360cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13{e}^{5}-9e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320097}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ec3dfe4a-5ecf-52b0-bf9f-2434195360cc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{13{e}^{5}-9e}{4}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320097} :answer-field/id #uuid "a761614e-3f16-5f4f-81d2-802676a5bea1" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320497}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "ddd7fba2-ad87-5331-9a58-1b0e4fa091f2" :question/math-academy-id "q-351994" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u={y}^{2}+4\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=2y, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}={e}^{2y+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y=\\int {e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y=\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =({y}^{2}+4)(\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2})(2y)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\int y\\,{e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int y\\,{e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=y\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}=1 \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}={e}^{2y+1}\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y=\\int {e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y=\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int y\\,{e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}y}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =(y)(\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2})-\\int (\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{2})(1)\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is a constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int ({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\int y\\,{e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}}{2}-(\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}+D) \\\\  & =\\frac{({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}}{2}-\\frac{y\\,{e}^{2y+1}}{2}+\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}-D \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}(2{y}^{2}-2y+9)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$ \n\nFinally then, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{2}({y}^{2}+4){e}^{2y+1}\\,\\text{d}y & ={[\\frac{{e}^{2y+1}}{4}(2{y}^{2}-2y+9)]}_{0}^{2} \\\\  & =(\\frac{{e}^{2(2)+1}}{4}(2(2)^{2}-2(2)+9))-(\\frac{{e}^{2(0)+1}}{4}(2(0)^{2}-2(0)+9)) \\\\  & =\\frac{{e}^{5}}{4}(8-4+9)-\\frac{e}{4}(0-0+9) \\\\  & =\\frac{13{e}^{5}}{4}-\\frac{9e}{4} \\\\  & =\\frac{13{e}^{5}-9e}{4}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320945 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "744e8198-302b-5b3f-865b-77ee955ad8cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319741}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "744e8198-302b-5b3f-865b-77ee955ad8cf" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "e-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319741} :answer-field/id #uuid "6d5038e6-e447-5d80-9e3f-bde4a002b130" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320392}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "c1d37f39-dd10-54e5-8b85-a29e10f1a31c" :question/math-academy-id "q-352040" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{1/3}^{e/3}3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=3 & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 3\\,\\text{d}x=3x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}(3x)-\\int (3x)(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-\\int 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=6 & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 6\\,\\text{d}x=6x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)(6x)-\\int (6x)(\\frac{1}{x})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-\\int 6\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-6x+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =3x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-\\int 6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =3x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-(6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)-6x+D) \\\\  & =3x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+6x-D \\\\  & =3x((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{1/3}^{e/3}3(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[3x((\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x))^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3x)+2)]}_{1/3}^{e/3} \\\\  & =[3(\\frac{e}{3})({(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{e}{3}))}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{e}{3})+2)]-[3(\\frac{1}{3})({(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3}))}^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot \\frac{1}{3})+2)] \\\\  & =[e((\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)]-[1((\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)^{2}-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2)] \\\\  & =[e(1-2+2)]-[1(0-0+2)] \\\\  & =e-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186320947 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068921 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "98a67346-c2c9-5d22-8adf-479fea81eaa2" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Logarithmic Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "14560af1-da74-5f36-a18b-b748d25b48a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{3}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319448}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "14560af1-da74-5f36-a18b-b748d25b48a9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "5{e}^{3}-2" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186319448} :answer-field/id #uuid "b77a2913-0af3-5bc9-8b65-91a3fcf636d6" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186320531}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "4d3bd2be-c388-5990-aa44-fdfa96c596f9" :question/math-academy-id "q-352056" :question/problem "Evaluate the following integral. Do not round the answer.\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{e-1}27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "We'll work out the indefinite integral first, and then proceed to work out the definite integral.\n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2} & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{x+1}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=27(x+1)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int 27(x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=9(x+1)^{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}(9(x+1)^{3})-\\int (9(x+1)^{3})(\\frac{2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-18\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe use integration by parts once again to evaluate $\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x.$ We take\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1) & \\,⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x+1}\\,, \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=(x+1)^{2} & \\,⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3}\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nIn this case, the formula for integration by parts gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)(\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3})-\\int (\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{3})(\\frac{1}{x+1})\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int (x+1)^{2}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{9}+D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $D$ is an integral constant. \n\nSo, we get that the indefinite integral is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\,\\text{d}x & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-18\\int (x+1)^{2}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-18(\\frac{(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)}{3}-\\frac{(x+1)^{3}}{9}+D) \\\\  & =9(x+1)^{3}{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-6(x+1)^{3}\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2(x+1)^{3}-18D \\\\  & =(x+1)^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2)+C,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=-18D.$\n\nFinally, we can work out the definite integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{e-1}27(x+1)^{2}(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))^{2}\\text{d}x & ={[(x+1)^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1))}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(x+1)+2)]}_{0}^{e-1} \\\\  & =[{e}^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡e)}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡e+2)]-[{1}^{3}(9{(\\operatorname{ln}⁡1)}^{2}-6\\operatorname{ln}⁡1+2)] \\\\  & =[{e}^{3}(9-6+2)]-2 \\\\  & =5{e}^{3}-2\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186332813 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186068920 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "d2a41ede-381e-55f6-99e5-1baf1ea348a1" :knowledge-point/title "Computing the Integral of a Trigonometric Expression Using Integration by Parts Twice"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2049d209-f71d-5036-a5ed-1c707b8cca2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{3}{8}\\int x\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330227} {:answer/id #uuid "2a1e5edf-0eb9-5389-987f-0ab7fb0a9f0e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(4x)}{3}+\\frac{5}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330322} {:answer/id #uuid "498eeb73-48b5-5b64-8c4a-7b75c33fa56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330609} {:answer/id #uuid "abb3132c-a6e4-5d7f-896f-2f57e4856624" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331473} {:answer/id #uuid "b1273dac-06c9-51ed-931a-5a5528347a85" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186331517}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "498eeb73-48b5-5b64-8c4a-7b75c33fa56e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186330609} :answer-field/id #uuid "e7be81ae-8223-53d0-8023-701f2e8a7fd5" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186332607}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "2e8ec66f-4e06-54fb-9f1e-056dedad6cc7" :question/math-academy-id "q-50226" :question/problem "$\\int (4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=$" :question/worked-solution "We need to use integration by parts. \n\nIn this case, we define\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}u=4{x}^{2}+1\\, & ⟹\\,\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=8x \\\\ \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}=\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\, & ⟹\\,v=\\int \\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x=\\int \\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x=-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the formula of integration by parts, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int (4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int u\\frac{\\text{d}v}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =uv-\\int v\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =(4{x}^{2}+1)(-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})-\\int (-\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3})(8x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{(4{x}^{2}+1)\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)}{3}+\\frac{8}{3}\\int x\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3x)\\,\\text{d}x.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
